{"text": " Last month, there were 58% more people trying to sell a house in America than there were people trying to buy one. That's the largest gap Redfin has seen since it started tracking this in 2013. By their measure, it's the strongest buyer's market on record. So you'd think that means home prices are falling. When sellers outnumber buyers in a market, prices are supposed to come down. Except they haven't. Last month, the typical American home sold for about $429,000. That's the 38th month in a row that prices were higher than the year before. Four in ten homeowners don't have a mortgage, which means no monthly debt payment, no bank to answer to, and no reason to sell their house unless they decide they want to. So homeowners are doing the only thing that makes financial sense, which is doing nothing. And if a home won't sell, we're seeing homeowners just pull the listing and wait. 45,000 homes that were pulled off the market last year came back in January alone, the most for any January in Redfin's records. It's the real estate version of deleting hinge, then re-downloading it a few months later, because you convinced yourself that this time it'll be different.", "segments": [{"id": 0, "seek": 0, "start": 0.0, "end": 5.96, "text": " Last month, there were 58% more people trying to sell a house in America than there were people trying to buy one.", "tokens": [50363, 4586, 1227, 11, 612, 547, 7618, 4, 517, 661, 2111, 284, 3677, 257, 2156, 287, 2253, 621, 612, 547, 661, 2111, 284, 2822, 530, 13, 50667], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.13715163799894958, "compression_ratio": 1.6193771626297577, "no_speech_prob": 0.0009761288529261947, "words": [{"word": " Last", "start": 0.0, "end": 0.4, "probability": 0.9307025074958801}, {"word": " month,", "start": 0.4, "end": 0.72, "probability": 0.9927043914794922}, {"word": " there", "start": 0.98, "end": 1.0, "probability": 0.989108681678772}, {"word": " were", "start": 1.0, "end": 1.2, "probability": 0.993032455444